WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.273
A biodiversity hotspot isn't

2
00:00:02.273 --> 00:00:03.791
just rich in species.

3
00:00:03.991 --> 00:00:05.997
It's defined by what it has lost.

4
00:00:06.497 --> 00:00:08.305
To qualify, a region needs

5
00:00:08.305 --> 00:00:10.253
at least 1,500 plant species

6
00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:11.574
found nowhere else,

7
00:00:11.694 --> 00:00:13.404
and must have lost at least

8
00:00:13.404 --> 00:00:15.050
70 percent of its original

9
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:15.747
vegetation.

10
00:00:16.197 --> 00:00:17.861
There are 36 worldwide.

11
00:00:18.011 --> 00:00:19.441
Four reach into India:

12
00:00:19.541 --> 00:00:21.067
the Himalaya, Indo-Burma,

13
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:22.165
the Western Ghats,

14
00:00:22.265 --> 00:00:23.731
and Sundaland, through the

15
00:00:23.731 --> 00:00:24.633
Nicobar Islands.

16
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:27.108
But a hotspot is a conservation

17
00:00:27.108 --> 00:00:28.219
label, not a law.

18
00:00:28.669 --> 00:00:30.356
Legal protection comes from

19
00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:32.167
the Wild Life Protection Act:

20
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.076
national parks, sanctuaries,

21
00:00:34.076 --> 00:00:35.691
conservation reserves and

22
00:00:35.691 --> 00:00:36.918
community reserves.

23
00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:39.000
In a national park, grazing

24
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:39.907
is not allowed.

25
00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:41.580
In a sanctuary, the Chief

26
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.112
Wildlife Warden can permit

27
00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:43.819
some rights.

28
00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:45.485
Rich, and threatened,

29
00:00:45.635 --> 00:00:46.851
by definition.

30
00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:48.247
Your turn.

31
00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:49.968
Which hotspot covers the

32
00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:50.915
Nicobar Islands?
